What is Enigma and why was it broken?\par
\par
The Enigma machine was a cipher device used by Nazi Germany during World War II to encrypt military communications, developed initially by Arthur Scherbius in 1918 for commercial use. It used rotating rotors and a plugboard to scramble messages, offering billions of possible settings, which the Germans believed made it unbreakable. A letter could never encrypt to itself, and settings changed daily, making decryption a monumental challenge.britannica.com\par
\par
It was broken primarily by Polish cryptographers (Marian Rejewski, Jerzy R\f0\'f3\f2\'bfycki, Henryk Zygalski) starting in 1932, using mathematical techniques and stolen manuals, and later by British codebreakers at Bletchley Park, including Alan Turing, who built the Bombe to automate decryption. The Poles cracked early versions by exploiting procedural flaws, like repeated message keys, and shared their findings with the Allies in 1939. Turing\rquote s Bombe used \ldblquote cribs\rdblquote  (guessed plaintext like \ldblquote weather report\rdblquote ) to narrow down rotor settings, cracking codes faster.en.wikipedia.orgiwm.org.uk\par
\par
The Enigma was broken to gain strategic advantage. Decrypting German messages (codenamed Ultra) revealed U-boat positions, troop movements, and plans, saving Allied convoys and shortening the war by years, potentially saving millions of lives. The effort was kept secret until the 1970s to protect cryptographic methods.cia.govbbc.com\par
